      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number of coins \\( N \\) that satisfies the following conditions:\n1. When \\( N \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 4. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n2. When \\( N \\) is divided by 5, the remainder is 3. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nFirst, let's express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nN = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\nNow, substitute this expression for \\( N \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSimplify the left side modulo 5:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[\nk \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince \\(-1\\) is congruent to 4 modulo 5, we have:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\nSubstitute this back into the expression for \\( N \\):\n\\[\nN = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28\n\\]\nThus, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nN = 30m + 28\n\\]\nThe smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), so:\n\\[\nN = 28\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 28 is divided by 7:\n\\[\n28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 0\n\\]\nTherefore, when 28 coins are equally divided among seven people, the number of coins left over is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
