      "text": "1. **Finding the smallest number of coins:**\n\n   Let \\( x \\) represent the smallest number of coins in the box. According to the problem, when the coins are divided among six people, four coins are left over. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, when the coins are divided among five people, three coins are left over. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solving the congruences:**\n\n   We need to find a number \\( x \\) that satisfies both congruences. We start with the first congruence:\n   \\[\n   x = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\( x = 6k + 4 \\) into the second congruence:\n   \\[\n   6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the congruence:\n   \\[\n   6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\implies 6k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\implies k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\implies k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( k \\) can be written as:\n   \\[\n   k = 5m + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into \\( x = 6k + 4 \\):\n   \\[\n   x = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28\n   \\]\n   Thus, the general solution for \\( x \\) is:\n   \\[\n   x \\equiv 28 \\pmod{30}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Finding the smallest positive \\( x \\):**\n\n   The smallest positive \\( x \\) that satisfies \\( x \\equiv 28 \\pmod{30} \\) is:\n   \\[\n   x = 28\n   \\]\n\n4. **Dividing the coins among seven people:**\n\n   We need to find the number of coins left when \\( x = 28 \\) is divided among seven people. Calculate:\n   \\[\n   28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore, when 28 coins are divided among seven people, there are:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0}\n   \\] coins left over.",
