      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number of gold coins that satisfies two conditions:\n1. When divided by 6, the remainder is 4.\n2. When divided by 5, the remainder is 3.\n\nLet's denote the number of coins by \\( N \\). The conditions can be expressed as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe start by writing \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side modulo 5, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nor equivalently:\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSo, we can write \\( k \\) as:\n\\[ k = 5m + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[ N = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nThus, the smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), so:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 28 coins are divided among 7 people:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ with a remainder of } 0 \\]\nSo, the number of coins left when equally divided among seven people is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
